## Why

Alchemy AgentPay now supports a proxy in front of an existing API, including x402 Testnet on **Solana Devnet**. OCM is a good adversarial canary because its hard cases are cancellation, provider failure/failover, and at-most-once usage/settlement rather than a simple one-shot API call.

Official references:
- https://agentpay.alchemy.com/docs
- https://agentpay.alchemy.com/

This is an integration/evaluation issue only. It does **not** claim an Alchemy partnership, production payment support, provider earnings, or production Solana settlement.

## Narrow test

Expose exactly one synthetic OCM inference route behind AgentPay x402 Testnet, with a fixed tiny test price and a Solana Devnet recipient.

Run a pinned request corpus through these cases:

1. successful non-streaming request;
2. successful stream through `[DONE]`;
3. client cancellation before completion;
4. selected provider fails before client commitment and OCM fails over;
5. backend failure after payment negotiation;
6. retry with the same logical request;
7. changed price / resource URL / recipient invalidates the prior payment authorization as AgentPay documents.

## Evidence to record

- exact OCM commit, model/runtime, AgentPay testnet channel and proxy path;
- 402 requirements returned before payment;
- whether payment verification/settlement happens before or after OCM knows the request produced usable output;
- whether cancellation or pre-commit failover can create a second buyer charge;
- how AgentPay and OCM each represent an ambiguous terminal outcome;
- one sanitized request/response trace for each case, with no keys, prompts, wallet secrets, or persistent host identifiers;
- observed devnet signatures only when they actually exist.

## Gate

Do not put AgentPay on a production route until the test establishes an explicit policy for paid-but-failed work, cancellation, retries, and OCM failover. OCM's internal usage ledger must remain independent evidence; a payment-provider receipt alone does not prove useful inference was delivered.

If the canary is clean, publish a short reproducible compatibility note and then approach Alchemy with the measured result rather than a generic partnership pitch.

Automated weekly sync from [x402-foundation/x402](https://github.com/x402-foundation/x402).

Upstream changed one or more example files. Our PayAI delta was reapplied
from `patches/` and the result passed:

- `check-examples.py` — every covered page still compiles
- `check_deltas.py` — facilitator, discovery and both networks intact
- `check-links.py` — no broken internal links

The live-payment gate runs on this PR. That is the one proving the
documented code actually takes a payment, not merely that it builds.

